{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Whether beast or man, it shall not live.</b> One should not understand 'beast' literally, exclusively; rather it is a designation for the wicked, who are compared to a beast. It says: whether he be wicked or righteous, the one who touches it shall not live. And thus Qohelet said (Ecclesiastes 3:21): ״מי יודע רוח בני האדם העולה היא למעלה ורוח הבהמה״ and so forth — he compared the soul of the wicked to a beast, and said: who knows them — that is, who recognizes them in this world, to say, this one will ascend and this one will descend? And he comes and teaches that in this world one fate befalls all, to the righteous and to the wicked: the death of this is like the death of that, and this is attached to the verse before it: ״כי הכל שב אל העפר״ — and in this world they are all equal, unlike in the world to come, where their ranks are distinct. And so it says here: ״לא יחיה״ — it made them all equal, whether righteous or wicked, that he shall not live in this world. And from this you learn that their ranks are distinct for the world to come — that is: the wicked shall not live in this world and in the world to come; and the righteous shall not live in this world but shall live in the world to come. And the proof is Nadav and Avihu, who died in this world because they touched the mountain concerning which they had been warned, yet they merited the life of the world to come — for this reason their death is recalled on the Day of Atonement." "<b>When the ram's horn is drawn out.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): when the ram's horn is drawn out — a long sound — it is a sign of the withdrawal of the Shekhinah and the cessation of the voice. The ram's horn (יובל) is the horn of a ram, and it was the ram's horn of Yitzhak, as Rashi, of blessed memory, explained. And his intention is to say that even though the ram was offered as a burnt-offering and was entirely consumed, the Holy One, blessed be He, restored it to its former existence. And thus our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded in Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer: Rabbi Ḥanina ben Dosa says: the ram that was created at twilight — nothing of it went to waste; its ashes are the foundation of the inner altar; its sinews are the ten strings of the harp with which David played; its skin is the belt around the waist of Elijah, remembered for good, as it is said (II Kings 1:8): ״ויאמרו אליו איש בעל שער ואזור עור אזור במתניו ויאמר אליה התשבי הוא״; and its left horn is the one heard at Mount Sinai, as it is said: ״ויהי קול השופר הולך״ and so forth; and its right horn, which is larger than the left, is the one that will be blown in the future to come, as it is said (Isaiah 27:13): ״והיה ביום ההוא יתקע בשופר גדול״ — end of quote. And from this verse our Rabbis, of blessed memory, proved in tractate Yom Tov, beginning of the first chapter: anything forbidden by a count requires another count to permit it — and this is what they said there in the Gemara: since it is written ״גם הצאן והבקר אל ירעו אל מול ההר ההוא״, why do I need ״במשוך היובל המה יעלו בהר״? From this we learn: anything forbidden by a count requires another count to permit it. And the explanation of this is: from the fact that it is written ״אל מול ההר ההוא״, one may learn: opposite that mountain — when the Shekhinah that was upon that mountain has withdrawn — they may graze. If so, why was it necessary to state ״במשוך היובל המה יעלו בהר״? Rather, in order to permit them by command, just as the prevention was by command — and from here, that anything forbidden by a count requires another count to permit it." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: במשוך היובל — the sound of this shofar is the awe of Yitzhak, which they grasped at Mount Sinai, and they grasped in it no speech (dibbur) but only sound (kol), and this is ״ויחרד כל העם״ — in the manner of (Genesis 27:33) ״ויחרד יצחק״. And I have already mentioned this in connection with the verse (Genesis 22:13): ״והנה איל אחר נאחז בסבך בקרניו״." "<b>And you shall set boundaries for the people.</b> Set a boundary for the people at the measure of two thousand cubits, whether less or more, so that they do not ascend the mountain and so that they do not touch its edge." "<b>Whoever touches the mountain shall surely be put to death.</b> All the more so one who ascends. <b>No hand shall touch him.</b> Now, has it not already been said ״כל הנוגע בהר״? But its explanation is: ״לא תגע בו יד״ — his death shall not be by the touching of a hand, such as strangulation or burning and the like, but from afar, such as stoning or with arrows; this is what it says: ״כי סקול יסקל או ירה יירה״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {2 "Lemma reads וע״ד, an abbreviation for ועל דרך; rendered as 'And by way of.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:01:18.801941879Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}